dots, changeable, logical) vs MAC (48-bit, colons, fixed, physical)."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}],["$","section","02-appearance",{"id":"02-appearance","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"How they look"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"An IP is 32 bits, four decimal octets separated by dots (192.168.1.4). A MAC is 48 bits, six hex bytes separated by colons or dashes (FA:34:AF:09:00:BA). Different lengths, different notation."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch08%2F02-appearance.jpg?v=8ac6833fdda5479f0e78cd44518e0b8a","alt":"IP 192.168.1.4 (32-bit, dots) vs MAC FA:34:AF:09:00:BA (48-bit, colons)."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}],["$","section","03-mutable",{"id":"03-mutable","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Changeable vs fixed"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":["$L25","$L26"]}]]}],"$L27","$L28","$L29","$L2a","$L2b"]}]
23:["$","$L2c",null,{"prev":{"slug":"mac-address-structure","title":"The Structure of a MAC Address","kind":"$undefined","number":7},"next":{"slug":"arp","title":"All About ARP","kind":"$undefined","number":9}}]
25:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"An IP can change, the router may hand your device a different one each time it connects. A MAC is hardcoded onto the NIC by the manufacturer and stays constant."}],["$","div","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Caveat",": "]}],"A MAC can be spoofed at the OS level, but normally it is fixed. IPs, by contrast, routinely change."]}]]}]]}]
26:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch08%2F03-mutable.jpg?v=024c943734085230bde69f7a5150d651","alt":"IP flips 192.168.1.4 to .9; MAC is baked into the NIC with a padlock."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
27:["$","section","04-uniqueness",{"id":"04-uniqueness","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Reusable vs universally unique"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The same IP can be reused in different networks. Two different homes, two separate isolated networks, both can have a device at 192.168.1.4, and that is fine because the networks never mix. A MAC, however, is globally unique: no two interfaces in the world share one. Even two NICs in the same laptop have different MACs."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch08%2F04-uniqueness.jpg?v=195afa1b16545385b6600cea2f40ce36","alt":"Two separate home networks both using 192.168.1.4; MACs never repeat."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
28:["$","section","05-ip-logical",{"id":"05-ip-logical","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"IP has logical meaning (routing)"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Say you request an EC2 instance by its public IP. Your subnet-mask check shows it is not local, so it goes to your gateway. That router checks whether the IP is in a network it knows; if not, it forwards to the next router (its own gateway), and so on, hop after hop, until it reaches the AWS data center."}],["$","div","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The point",": "]}],"At every hop the IP is used to make a routing decision. IP has logical significance, you can compute where to send next from it."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch08%2F05-ip-logical.jpg?v=60df4a12d61fa5acd382346dc845cc2b","alt":"Laptop through several routers to an AWS data center EC2; IP used to decide the next hop at every router."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
29:["$","section","06-mac-physical",{"id":"06-mac-physical","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"MAC is for physical delivery"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A MAC address is essentially random, there is no logic in it to route by. But once the packet finally reaches the destination’s local network, the MAC is exactly what is needed to hand the data to the one specific machine whose interface matches."}],["$","div","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Scope",": "]}],"MAC only matters inside the local network, for the last step of delivery to the exact device."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch08%2F06-mac-physical.jpg?v=a2c6343f6efe531b31445bce21390b51","alt":"On the destination network, the packet is delivered to the exact device whose MAC matches."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
2a:["$","section","07-arp-teaser",{"id":"07-arp-teaser","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"07"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"ARP bridges IP → MAC"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"So how does a device get the MAC for a given IP when it needs to deliver locally? With ARP (Address Resolution Protocol), which takes an IP and returns the matching MAC. Every device keeps an ARP table mapping IPs to MACs, the subject of the next chapter."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch08%2F07-arp-teaser.jpg?v=fb4eecd4bbbee811147458f160e6e164","alt":"IP resolved to MAC via ARP, with an IP|MAC ARP table."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
2b:["$","section","08-division-of-labour",{"id":"08-division-of-labour","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"08"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The division of labour"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Put simply: IP is globally significant and gets your data across the internet to the right local network. MAC works only inside that local network to reach the exact device. Neither can do the other’s job, which is why we need both."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch08%2F08-division-of-labour.jpg?v=6f8acd5b9895788107557763d9935195","alt":"IP carries you across the internet; MAC delivers to the exact device at the end."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
